SM(544,{"n":"1 Corinthians 12","a":"Fr Stephen De Young Archives/1 Corinthians 12.mp3","d":2789.9,"s":[[0.16,"Come and study holy scriptures with us as Father Stephen DeYoung teaches verse by verse on the podcast The Whole Counsel of God."],[8.08,"Father Stephen is a former Protestant pastor and Bible teacher in the Reformed Church, but now an Orthodox priest, serving at Archangel Gabriel Orthodox Church in Lafayette, Louisiana."],[22.24,"So we'll go ahead and get started, and we're gonna get started in just a moment."],[26.64,"We'll be picking up with St."],[28.88,"Paul's first epistle to the Corinthians, chapter 12, right at the beginning."],[35.12,"Chapter 12, verse 1."],[37.36,"And we uh left off a little early last time because we kind of got here, and this is sort of a major break in that St."],[46.08,"Paul is sort of now shifting topics."],[48.72,"We just finished a sort of large section where St."],[53.12,"Paul was talking about worship, and that mainly focused on the Eucharist, then other related things that had to do with mostly contrasting, though in a supple couple cases comparing the worship of the church centered in the Eucharist with the pagan worship in Corinth, out of which the Christians in Corinth had come, or needed to come, as the case may be, because some of them were still sort of half in the pagan world and half in the Christian world, and were trying to sort of do both at the same time."],[90.28,"So, in some cases, similarities meant that they needed to stop."],[94.56,"Because, for example, as St."],[96.04,"Paul pointed out, both the Eucharist and the pagan sacrifices were doing the same thing in the sense of connecting a community together. and connecting that community to a God who was being worshipped"],[109.6,"And so you couldn't worship both demons and God at the same time."],[114.8,"And so they had to stop participating in both."],[118.16,"And sometimes the differences meant they had to stop participating in both."],[122.96,"Because one of the other related themes was the sexual immorality that was very common in pagan worship at the time."],[130.8,"And so, because that sexual immorality was unacceptable for Christians, that was another reason why they needed to remove themselves from from uh the world of uh pagan worship."],[143.52,"Even though that meant that meant a real separation between them as a Christian commun comitymunity and the rest of the city as a."],[153.8,"Because as we've had all their social cause."],[156.46,"Because as we talked about before, you know, individual families had gods who they felt themselves obligated to serve, which Christians would have to, which would require Christians to break some of the ties with their families."],[172.78,"The cities, of course, had gods who they served that they were gonna have to break ties with in terms of the public festivals and the city events that they'd have to break."],[181.98,"And this even extended to, as we talked about, what they could eat, since almost all of the meat in the meat market in Corinth came from the pagan temples and had come out of the sacrifices."],[193.1,"So this meant they were really ha going to have to to really fulfill what they were being called to do as a Christian community."],[202.18,"They were going to have to become their own sort of independent community."],[206.74,"And so much of the rest of what we're going to read in 1 Corinthians is going to be dealing with that element."],[216.02,"So we've seen sort of so far coming up to chapter 12, a lot of the what you might call the negative element of these are the things you need to stop doing."],[224.78,"These are the things that need to change."],[227.4,"And now we're gonna shift to more of more of the positive element of here's what this new community that we're forming needs to be like in a positive sense."],[239.32,"And here's what your identity is as Christians, and here's the identity of this community, and how to work that out."],[248.2,"Because of course, just saying be separate is one thing, but now you've got this group of people that's separated from all the sort of forms of authority and the culture in which they formerly existed."],[264.2,"They're gonna have to, just like any other group, they're gonna have to form some kind of culture now and some kind of structure for themselves in order to function and provide for themselves as the community of the church."],[277.0,"And so that's really what St."],[279.16,"Paul is going to transition to here now as we go forward."],[284.12,"So unless we have any leftover questions or comments or yes, ma'am"],[290.48,"Last time we talked about how we shouldn't approach the Eucharist in an unworthily manner."],[296.83,"What is unworthily matter?"],[299.79,"Well as we mentioned at least briefly we do it worthily when we approach it in repentance where we've confessed our sins, where there's not some grievance between us and another person when we approach recognizing that we aren't we aren't worthy of it in the sense of an adjective."],[320.91,"It's not that okay, now I'm holy and pure, and so now I can come and receive communion"],[326.29,"If you think you're holy and pure, then you're not qualified."],[330.34,"But that you realize you're not holy and that you need to be purified."],[335.0,"And that's what you're coming to receive, that you're coming to receive Christ."],[339.0,"So those are the main, those are the main things that St."],[342.68,"Paul is focusing on."],[344.36,"And in particular, particular problem, of course, in Corinth was related to the fact that the Eucharist was happening in the context of a meal and they were treating the meal like a pagan feast."],[355.8,"So they were getting drunk and overindulging in food and all of these things."],[361.08,"And so that is definitely not the worthy, sober, way that he was that he was talking about it."],[369.09,"I keep telling you, Father, we could increase church intention."],[371.17,"We got a potluck afterwards"],[373.61,"We just had lots of drinks and so forth."],[378.01,"I mean, a potluck, you know."],[380.65,"That's close. You know, it's the sedate Middle America version of a feast."],[387.85,"Okay, so 1 Corinthians uh chapter 12, verse 1."],[393.69,"Now, concerning spiritual gifts, brethren, I do not want you to be ignorant."],[398.89,"So St. Paul sort of has a format for how he's starting these sections."],[404.25,"It's been a while, so you may not remember, but at the beginning of the last section, at the beginning of chapter 8, he said, now concerning things offered to idols, it was the same phrase."],[415.95,"So that's sort of the way he's marking his transitions."],[419.95,"So one topic, now concerning this other topic."],[424.91,"So that's him indicating the shift."],[426.75,"And so what he's going to be talking about here."],[429.15,"Now you notice gifts there is in italics."],[433.31,"That means it doesn't exist."],[434.83,"That means that word is not there."],[436.59,"Now that's good."],[437.71,"The reason they're supplying gifts there is going to come out in terms of as we go forward in the in the text, that that's what St."],[446.11,"Paul is talking about."],[447.31,"But what it literally means is spiritual things, you could translate it, but one mistake that gets made in a lot of English translations of the New Testament is that almost every time, nothing is true 100% of the time, but almost every time the word spirit is used in the New Testament, it's referring to the Holy Spirit."],[471.27,"We have this way of using the word spiritual in English that is misleading."],[478.89,"In the sense that we talk about being spiritual or spiritual things, and that might have no reference to the Holy Spirit at all."],[486.51,"We're just talking about sort of being religious or being pious or or and then you get the people who say they're not religious, they're spiritual, and that's even more confusing."],[495.87,"But but uh when when it's used in the scriptures, really in the old testament as well, it's being used either things of the spirit.irit of God in the old testament, things of the Holy Sp, and of course it's the same person in the New Testament"],[512.07,"It's really referring to the things, the things of the spirit."],[515.77,"The only time it's not, it usually has another adjective in front of it, like an unclean spirit."],[521.37,"Then you know it's not talking about the Holy Spirit, it's talking about a demon."],[525.54,"Or a lying spirit, or a right."],[528.1,"There'll be some other adjective to indicate to you that this is not the spirit of God or the Holy Spirit."],[534.36,"And so when you see just what might be transferred translated spiritual, it almost always should have a capital F."],[542.44,"So, really, a better translation of this would be now concerning the things of the spirit."],[547.82,"The things of the spirit, the meaning the Holy Spirit."],[550.66,"And so this is the topic broadly that he's turning to."],[553.94,"And he says, I do not want you to be ignorant."],[556.66,"So some of the things he talked about, remember, for example, when he talked about the Eucharist that we let read last time, he said, What I received from Christ, I passed on to you."],[568.66,"So this was stuff he had taught them during his previous visits."],[572.76,"He taught them how to celebrate the Eucharist."],[574.92,"He taught them these things."],[576.44,"When he says here, I do not want you to be ignorant, this means this is something that he at least, now we know other people, Apollos has come to other people have come there and taught, but that St."],[586.92,"Paul at least has not taught them about before."],[589.64,"So this I do not want you to be ignorant is sort of his way of saying I need to teach you some more on this topic."],[596.58,"So concerning the things of the Spirit, I need to teach you a little bit more, is what he's saying."],[601.78,"You know that you were Gentiles, carried away to these dumb idols, however you were led."],[609.54,"Now, dumb is not stupid."],[612.3,"Dumb means not speaking."],[614.38,"Not speaking. And so this is part of why it's important that he's talking about the Holy Spirit, in that he's making a contrast here between the idols that were worshipped before, and the Holy Spirit now, who has spoken."],[630.77,"But however you were led, does that mean whoever? told you what to do"],[636.81,"Right. However, you ended up there."],[638.68,"However, you ended up there, this is where you ended up."],[641.24,"Okay. That was the case before."],[643.64,"And so there's there's this contrast going on."],[645.8,"And remember, this is going all the way back to something he said."],[648.6,"Remember the first couple of chapters, we said he was he St."],[651.32,"Paul always sort of lays out the themes he's gonna talk about through the rest of the letter."],[656.2,"Remember, he taught compared sort of you're looking to these these other gods and these idols and these temples and these rituals to try and get you some kind of wisdom, remember?"],[667.8,"And he said that's that's not where wisdom is."],[670.2,"They don't know anything."],[671.89,"All the wisdom of God has already been given to you when you received the Holy Spirit."],[676.85,"He said that all the way back in chapter one."],[679.13,"And so he's sort of coming back to that here."],[681.71,"So you are you are worshiping these idols who do not speak, who did not tell you or teach you anything."],[687.49,"That just sat there."],[689.25,"Verse three, therefore I make known to you that no one speaking by the Spirit of God calls Jesus accursed, and no one can say that Jesus is Lord except by the Holy Spirit."],[699.09,"So um he's telling them, remember, he said, however, you were led, you were led captive to serving those idols."],[706.87,"Well, here's a test to make sure you don't get led back."],[709.91,"If someone comes to you and says they have some spiritual wisdom to tell you, he's already established that the only all the only spiritual wisdom, all of the wisdom of God, is in the Holy Spirit."],[722.57,"So if someone really has wisdom to tell you, they're going to be speaking by the Holy Spirit."],[726.89,"So if they then ask the next question, well, how do we know that this person who's talking to us is speaking from the Holy Spirit?"],[734.17,"He says, Well, this is how."],[747.85,"Yes. Is that saying Jesus is God?"],[751.53,"Or is that saying Jesus is the authority?"],[755.57,"In context, so we have to we have to take the context."],[760.21,"Why would someone say that Jesus is accursed?"],[764.13,"If they were opposed to him altogether."],[768.17,"What but what specifically would put him under a curse?"],[772.49,"Being crucified. Being crucified."],[775.46,"Meaning. He's talking about a particular group of teachers."],[781.12,"He's not just talking in general."],[782.96,"Ah, okay. Now remember, these are people who show up claiming to speak by the Spirit of God."],[788.18,"Would Gentiles show up?"],[790.66,"Would pagans show up?"],[792.34,"These would be Jews."],[793.4,"These would be Jewish teachers."],[795.4,"Because remember, there's not Judaism and Christianity as two separate religions."],[800.45,"We're in the 50s AD."],[803.11,"So synagogues have both people who have accepted Jesus as the Messiah and people who haven't."],[809.49,"And you didn't get sort of a card from the apostles saying you're licensed, you're a licensed evangelist."],[815.43,"Or something. So anybody who shows up and claims they're teaching by the Spirit of God, so the one who isn't, the one who doesn't have the Holy Spirit, is the one who would say that Jesus is accursed."],[828.97,"And remember what we said last time, Saint Paul very rarely uses the name Jesus."],[836.73,"He usually says Christ or the Lord."],[839.69,"When he I mean he's talking about Jesus, but he usually uses the title."],[843.45,"So when he uses the name Jesus, he's specifically referring to Jesus in his earthly life and his earthly ministry."],[851.01,"So the last time he used it, he was talking about the Eucharist."],[854.21,"He said Jesus on the night he was betrayed."],[856.54,"So he was talking about these specific events."],[859.66,"Okay, so he's saying the same thing."],[861.18,"Someone who comes up and says Jesus is under a curse, which would be a Jewish teacher who did not accept Jesus as the Messiah, who said that he died under a curse because he died hanging on a tree."],[872.8,"That person does not have the Holy Spirit, is what St."],[875.92,"Paul's saying. And then the the flip side of that, would be someone who comes and says, Jesus is Lord."],[884.66,"That shows that they do have the Holy Spirit, that they confess that Jesus is Lord."],[889.46,"So again we have the name Jesus, and you notice he doesn't say."],[893.88,"If he meant Lord like ruler, what would he have said?"],[898.0,"He would have said, No one can say that Jesus is Christ."],[902.1,"No one could say Jesus is the Christ."],[903.7,"No one could say Jesus is the Messiah."],[905.38,"Uh-huh. Which he could have said."],[907.78,"But that's not what he said."],[909.54,"So no one could say Jesus is Lord."],[912.18,"No one can say Jesus is Kyrios in in Greek."],[917.06,"The importance of that is that Kyrios is what almost all the time."],[923.62,"Lots of almost all the times tonight, but almost all the time in the Greek uh translations of the Old Testament, Kyrios is what is put in to replace the name Yahweh."],[935.79,"That's what I was speaking here."],[937.31,"Yeah. Kyrgios is put in to replace the name Yahweh."],[939.47,"Remember that St"],[940.79,"And we already saw back in chapter six."],[946.23,"Paul took the Shema, the prayer, from Deuteronomy, Hero Israel, the Lord your God, the Lord is one, or actually in the Hebrew, Hero Israel, Yahweh your God, Yahweh is one."],[959.51,"But Kyrios. Lord in the Greek, he took that and he inserted Jesus' name into it."],[966.57,"He said, We have one God the Father and one Lord Jesus Christ."],[970.57,"So he inserted both people."],[972.33,"So he's already set this up earlier in First Corinthians, that that's how he's using the term Lord."],[977.85,"He's using the term Kyrios from the Old Testament."],[981.21,"So, yes, what he's saying is, except by the Holy Spirit, no one can acknowledge that Jesus is God, is Yahweh is the God of Israel."],[993.25,"Is Yahweh. That's the mark for him of someone who has the Holy Spirit."],[996.85,"Because here for Saint Paul, what separates Christians from everybody else is having received the Holy Spirit."],[1004.21,"It's the baptism of the Holy Spirit that makes them a Christian, not anything else."],[1008.61,"Yeah, so that is, those are the two things."],[1011.97,"And there's also, it's probably not a reference here, because uh Saint Matthew's gospel, for example, hadn't been written yet."],[1021.17,"So it's not that St."],[1022.61,"Paul is necessarily drawing on this story, but there's a connection in ideas here."],[1027.09,"Remember St. Peter's confession."],[1028.77,"But Christ is Lord"],[1032.01,"You are the Christ, the Son of the living God."],[1034.75,"That he said to Jesus."],[1037.07,"And Jesus says, flesh and blood is not revealed this to you, but the Holy Spirit has revealed it to you."],[1044.83,"What Christ is saying there is not, oh, the Holy Spirit put this truth in your head."],[1051.33,"Or whispered it in your ear."],[1052.77,"He's saying the Holy Spirit allowed you to see who I am."],[1055.81,"Uh-huh. Because that was the St."],[1057.97,"Peter's what St."],[1059.01,"Peter was answering Christ's question, who do you say that I am?"],[1064.43,"And he's saying the Holy Spirit has allowed you to see."],[1067.4,"This is true, and the Holy Spirit has allowed you to see it."],[1070.2,"So when St."],[1071.32,"Paul says, no one can say it, it's not like if someone who didn't have the Holy Spirit tried to lie and tried to say it like they was you know they couldn't get the words out of their mouth."],[1082.94,"What he means by by no one can say it is the only way that you can know this to be true is to have the Holy Spirit dwelling within you."],[1092.54,"And so that's why this is the test."],[1096.7,"So verse 4."],[1098.14,"There are diversities of gifts, but the same spirit."],[1102.86,"There are differences of ministries, but the same Lord."],[1106.38,"And there are diversities of activities, but is the same God who works in all."],[1111.9,"So now that he's established."],[1114.56,"What makes you a Christian."],[1116.06,"What makes you a member of this community that's set apart is that you have the Holy Spirit dwelling within you."],[1121.84,"He's returned to that idea."],[1123.84,"Now he's saying, within that, you've all received the Holy Spirit, but that doesn't mean you're all identical now."],[1130.69,"You're not all the same person."],[1132.75,"So there are different things going on with each of you."],[1136.19,"But notice the structure here."],[1138.67,"In verse 4, he says, but the same Spirit."],[1143.47,"In verse 5, but the same Lord."],[1145.87,"And in verse 6, but the same God."],[1149.55,"So he's now once again laid out the Trinity."],[1156.01,"Right there. So we've got the Spirit, Jesus is the Lord, God the Father."],[1159.05,"And so I'm just noting this as we go through First Corinthians that St"],[1166.03,"Paul is very clearly Trinitarian over and over again."],[1172.03,"This isn't something that gets uh made up later."],[1175.71,"It's embedded here in just the way he's speaking, because he says those three things in direct parallel."],[1181.95,"So, and the three things there are is there's different gifts, there's different ministries, and there's different activities."],[1188.67,"Okay, so they have different abilities."],[1192.37,"They have different ways to use those abilities in the community, and because of that, the word activities here is really the same concept that we we talk about in the Orthodox Church when talk about the divine energies or God's energies."],[1208.97,"That's why St."],[1209.77,"Paul says it's the same God who is working in all."],[1225.69,"Energy literally means in working."],[1227.78,"Activities is actually a good kind of a good translation."],[1231.3,"God is at work in his creation, all in all of his creation."],[1234.66,"That's what the all in all is referring to."],[1237.3,"God's at work in all of his creation, including in us."],[1240.52,"And we participate in that."],[1242.44,"And St. Paul is saying we all participate in that in different ways and in different parts of what God is doing."],[1249.3,"But it's the same God who's doing all of it."],[1252.36,"It's not that we're somehow separated."],[1255.56,"So the theme he's hammering on all three times is."],[1259.9,"We have different abilities, we're doing different things, we're participating in different parts of God's project."],[1266.91,"But we're all one in the same way that the Father and the Son and the Spirit are all one."],[1274.51,"Okay, so this is his sort of bird's eye view of the community."],[1280.17,"We're all doing our separate parts, but we're all one together, because we're all one with the same God."],[1287.13,"Someplace else he says much the same thing, but more spilled out, you know, where not all are."],[1290.01,"He's getting that's in a few uh that's in a few months"],[1297.09,"Okay. You're anticipating."],[1299.57,"Yeah. So verse 7."],[1302.21,"But the manifestation of the spirit is given to each one for the profit of all."],[1307.41,"So this is an important point he's making."],[1310.15,"We all do these different things."],[1311.59,"We all have these different gifts."],[1313.43,"But the gifts that I've been given are not for my benefit."],[1318.89,"They're not so that I'm special."],[1321.77,"I'm a beautiful and unique snowflake."],[1324.03,"It's not because of that."],[1325.71,"It's they're given to me to benefit everyone else."],[1328.91,"And the gifts that you're given are given to you to benefit everyone else."],[1331.88,"And and everyone else so that by all of us doing our part, the whole community benefits."],[1338.66,"So it isn't just we're all unique and special in our own way, and isn't that nice, but we all have this role to play in terms of everyone else in the community."],[1348.98,"For to one is given the word of wisdom through the spirit, to another the word of knowledge through the same spirit."],[1355.48,"So there's wisdom and knowledge we tend to think of as the same thing."],[1358.84,"But they're not."],[1359.58,"But they're not."],[1360.3,"You could ask my wife, I've got a PhD, I do not have a lot of wisdom."],[1364.54,"Uh that's she sometimes says I'm the dumbest smart person she's ever met."],[1369.58,"Uh so these are two different things sometimes."],[1373.2,"But and he's continuing his theme that all real wisdom com comeses thro thughro theugh the spir spitir.it, and all real knowledge"],[1380.04,"So but there are some people who have accumulated a great deal of knowledge."],[1385.73,"And the purpose of that is not for them to sit around and think about how smart they are or to think great thoughts, but so that they can pass on that knowledge to the rest of the community."],[1395.09,"There's another person who maybe doesn't have a lot of knowledge in terms of being able to quote you things and explain things and you know tell you history and and that sort of thing, but has lived a long life, maybe a hard life, and has learned a lot of lessons and can give very good advice and discernment to people."],[1413.11,"And has this wisdom."],[1414.39,"It also comes from the spirit."],[1415.99,"But they didn't receive that so that they could go sit on a mountain and and you know deep thoughts."],[1423.15,"They received that so they could so they could benefit benefit the community."],[1428.99,"Just as a side note, this is why even in the the Orthodox monastic tradition that has to do with monks who are hermits, there are points where even hermits like Saint Anthony in the desert took disciples."],[1452.27,"But then they pass them on."],[1453.39,"There's a point where St."],[1454.19,"Seraphim Muserov out by himself in the woods, you know, in Russia, opened the doors of his cell and started to receive people."],[1462.45,"So even in those cases, and even in the cases where the monastic is at a place where they don't have a lot of direct contact, you know, and even if they don't aren't sending a lot of letters, which many of them did, at a certain point begin to correspond, their prayers are reaching out for the whole the whole church."],[1481.91,"Monasticism, even in the form of hermits, is not an exception to what St."],[1488.09,"Paul is saying here about the church that gifts are given for the sake of the whole church."],[1493.37,"And God gives saints to the church as a gift to benefit the whole church."],[1499.77,"To another faith by the same spirit, to another gifts of healings by the same spirit, to another the working of miracles, to another prophecy, to another discerning of spirits, to another and again different is added there, to another kinds of tongues, to another the interpretation of tongues."],[1517.41,"But one and the same spirit works all these things, distributing to each one individually as he wills."],[1523.01,"Okay, so he gives these he gives these other examples."],[1526.29,"Of course, faith we have to remember is more like the idea of faithfulness."],[1529.97,"It's not that, oh, that guy believes really, really hard."],[1533.25,"That's a gift."],[1535.41,"Whereas somebody else doesn't believe it."],[1537.54,"It's it's it's faithfulness of their life."],[1540.26,"And there are saints who we can look at and look at their life."],[1543.54,"You know, the martyrs who are faithful even unto their death and that kind of thing."],[1547.22,"So there there are these different gifts."],[1548.74,"There's saints who are wonder workers."],[1550.88,"There are saints who a note on tongues."],[1554.32,"Yes, I was gonna ask."],[1555.4,"Because of course everyone insists on, I guess because of the King James Version, everyone still insists on, even in the Orthodox Study Bible, translating this as tongues."],[1565.64,"Tongue is, I guess, wouldly literally what the Greek word is."],[1569.56,"But of course it means languages."],[1571.4,"So all it's saying is some people have a gift for speaking lots of languages."],[1576.44,"We've probably all know people who know a whole bunch of languages and are just really fluent in them."],[1582.41,"And the interpretation of tongues is being able to understand a lot of languages and translate them."],[1589.05,"Because being able to speak a language, just because you could speak two languages doesn't mean you're very good at translating."],[1595.15,"Those are separate skills."],[1597.65,"And separ seateparate gifts."],[1600.49,"There are people who interpret this as speaking in tongues in the sense of saying incomprehensible things."],[1609.06,"And somebody else says, oh, well, what that incomprehensible noise meant is that."],[1616.36,"Which is kind of like the the oracle at Delphi."],[1619.48,"Yeah, because again, St."],[1620.84,"Paul's whole thrust here is what?"],[1624.36,"Is these gifts are given to you for the benefit of everyone else."],[1628.5,"So you speaking in some uh sort of babbling language, and then another person having to translate that into the regular language that everyone speaks."],[1639.02,"How is that to everyone's benefit, other than as a parlor trick?"],[1641.94,"How does that help the community"],[1643.18,"No, I'm being honest."],[1648.7,"All these other gifts I could I could see how they help the community."],[1653.27,"That one I don't see."],[1654.39,"Whereas if it's just talking about one person can speak a lot of languages and another person can translate languages, I could see how that would be of great benefit to the community."],[1663.83,"Especially a community like Corinth that remembers at the place where the Peloponnese meets the mainland of Greece that was a port, and the land route passed through there, so you'd have Romans who spoke Latin, you'd have, I mean, Greek was the main language, but you've also got people speaking Aramaic."],[1682.73,"Of a Jewish background, you've got people from other places."],[1685.73,"There were still local languages."],[1688.21,"We don't really appreciate this, but it wasn't just Aramaic."],[1692.45,"We know about Aramaic because of the Gospels."],[1695.62,"Because Galilee, Judea, they spoke Aramaic."],[1697.78,"But there were native languages everywhere."],[1699.78,"They spoke Egyptian."],[1701.3,"Most of the common people spoke Egyptian in Egypt, still."],[1706.66,"What became Coptic, but they spoke Egyptian, even though Greek was the official language."],[1712.02,"Aramaic turned into Syriac, like in Syria."],[1714.9,"That's what the common people spoke."],[1716.74,"The wealthy people, the Romans, spoke Greek."],[1719.62,"And so you will find things during this time and later where people will talk about people like in Syria, like Tatian will talk about the Greeks."],[1729.68,"tal He'ks.ing about essentially the ruling class in the third century, the Roman ruling class were the Greeks"],[1737.16,"And this was true, this was true in Galatia."],[1739.56,"We're going to read Galatians soon."],[1741.56,"Galatia, there were still native languages."],[1743.96,"Greek was spoken by the Roman settlers there, but the natives had their own language."],[1748.2,"So there was this great amount of linguistic diversity that we don't necessarily appreciate."],[1754.2,"And so if you were in a major cosmopolitan city like Corinth or Alexandria or Jerusalem or Rome or Antioch."],[1763.86,"And you were part of the Christian community, being able to speak to visitors who didn't necessarily know Greek was very helpful to the community."],[1771.94,"Being able to translate for somebody, very helpful to the community."],[1774.02,"Being able to preach to people in the language in their local language"],[1780.86,"This is all very these are very useful gifts to the to the whole community."],[1786.38,"And so I think part of it is that we don't appreciate how linguistically diverse the world still was at this point, especially when you take social class into account."],[1795.18,"It's like, yeah, everyone who's educated knew Greek."],[1797.74,"It's like, yeah, everyone who's educated."],[1800.22,"And who were most of the early Christian converts?"],[1803.42,"We've talked about this before, too."],[1805.34,"Mostly women and slaves who weren't mostly well, slaves weren't allowed at all."],[1811.26,"Women, most women were not allowed to get formal educations."],[1815.84,"So they didn't speak a bunch of languages."],[1818.08,"So this is a very important, very important gift"],[1824.44,"So when the community of people speaking together for the Eucharist, the service is in Greek, but there are people who don't understand."],[1833.72,"Probably. Probably. It's probably in whatever the majority language was."],[1837.56,"Yeah. So in Corinth, that would presumably be Greek."],[1840.76,"Yeah. And so there would be people who wouldn't necessarily which is written in Greek."],[1847.78,"Would have to be translated."],[1849.43,"Uh and explained in other languages, potentially."],[1852.23,"And that's not to say that there was never a miraculous element to this."],[1856.95,"Because we do have an example in the New Testament of people speaking a miraculous language."],[1862.39,"But it was the exact opposite of what people call speaking in tongues today."],[1863.51,"It was Pentecost"],[1868.91,"The apostles preached and everyone understood them."],[1873.77,"So for an interpret the opposite."],[1877.75,"The exact opposite."],[1879.35,"Um so there may have been this may include, because this is in the context of talking about people who work miracles and people who do other things like that."],[1888.15,"This may have sometimes been miraculous."],[1890.79,"Someone being able to speak a language that they didn't actually know in order to communicate the gospel to someone or to to connect with someone."],[1901.03,"So there may be a miraculous element to it."],[1903.35,"But even the miraculous element is not me speaking in some made up fictional language."],[1912.49,"Now that I've alienated my probably non existent Pentecostal audience for the podcast."],[1920.73,"And so, but again, St."],[1922.25,"Paul reemphasizes there in verse 11, it's one and the same spirit who's doing all of these things and giving to each person individually as the spirit wills."],[1931.87,"And again, notice he why is he saying as the spirit wills?"],[1937.23,"Well, because part of the problem he's been correcting since chapter one is these people have come from the pagan world where you go out and have these initiation experiences in the mystery.ies of Demeter and Corinth or these other things in order to tr to acquire this sort of spiritual knowledge and stuff"],[1958.71,"So this isn't you go out and ascend, you know, to the next level of Thetans in Scientology or something, and now you can alter time and space, like Tom Cruise."],[1970.23,"This is right, the Holy Spirit gives it to you as he wills to give to each person."],[1977.27,"Verse 12 for as the body is one and has many members, but all the members of that one body being many are one body, so also is Christ."],[1988.59,"Okay, so he's he's now beginning an analogy, and it's gonna be a protracted analogy."],[1993.56,"Where he says it's sort of like your body has. a whole bunch of different parts"],[1999.21,"And even though they're all different parts and they don't look like each other."],[2003.39,"My big toe doesn't look like my nose, thankfully."],[2007.2,"And my my ears, you know, don't look like my thumbs."],[2010.72,"But they're all part of one body."],[2012.96,"And he's saying Christ is the same way."],[2015.36,"Now notice when he says Christ is the same way, he's been talking about the community in the church."],[2020.56,"So he's already introducing the idea of the church being the body of Christ."],[2025.6,"Yeah, that is a shift, isn't it?"],[2027.6,"Yeah. He doesn't say, so you've got a body and it's made up of a whole bunch of parts, but it's one body, so also is the church, or so also is your community."],[2038.22,"He says, so also is Christ."],[2040.62,"For by one spirit we were all baptized into one body, whether Jews or Greeks, whether slaves or free, and have all been made to drink into one spirit, for in fact the body is not one member but many."],[2055.58,"So you notice how he talks about baptism."],[2059.02,"He's talked about water baptism already."],[2060.78,"Remember, he went back to Moses."],[2062.38,"But whenever he talks about water baptism, he also talks about the Holy Spirit immediately afterwards."],[2068.78,"We all were baptized and we all received the Holy Spirit, because it's particularly, as he's been saying, receiving the Holy Spirit that makes you a Christian in particular."],[2079.74,"And this is why baptism and chrismation remain united in the Orthodox Church."],[2083.26,"These are connected things"],[2088.34,"Whereas in the West, they got separated."],[2091.62,"And you got baptism and then confirmation sometime later."],[2097.07,"We've kept them together."],[2099.07,"Drink made to drink into one spirit."],[2101.71,"That seems like a very strange formulation."],[2105.07,"Yeah, it's more like drink in."],[2107.71,"One spirit. Yeah."],[2110.09,"The preposition, I don't know how much I want to go into this."],[2113.05,"Me Just to say, the pro Greek preposition east that's translated into can also just mean to."],[2121.29,"Uh-huh. It actually shifted in meaning."],[2124.41,"It went from meaning in to meaning into to meaning to over the course."],[2127.13,"ing to. And it was in the process in the first century of switch from from into in"],[2135.01,"So yeah, so they're being really literal by translating it into, but it really just means really just means in."],[2143.73,"And then later, this is just a total trivia note."],[2147.01,"Later it shifted even more."],[2148.77,"It went from into to in, and then it changed to just two, to two or towards."],[2155.81,"And that's actually where part of where uh the name East Endbowl comes from."],[2162.29,"Uh-huh. They asked this is literally true."],[2166.69,"Some Turk pointed it Constantinople."],[2170.55,"And said Where's Where's that"],[2170.71,"And said they to the city, which is Istanbulis."],[2178.05,"And of course, famously, most Middle Eastern people turn P's into B's."],[2183.09,"It happens all the time in language, and so it became East Eastan Bolis and then Istanbul."],[2190.69,"Uh-huh. Yeah. So Istanbul literally means to the city."],[2197.01,"That's okay. I don't recognize that anyway."],[2199.17,"Yeah. Just like I still call it New Amsterdam."],[2203.49,"But yeah, so that's why that is weird."],[2206.61,"Because I knew I'm into the city, but I didn't."],[2209.01,"Yeah, but so that's why the into one spirit to drink into."],[2213.89,"And he just uses a say so into makes sense."],[2216.61,"You could say we were baptized in one body"],[2216.97,"We're baptized into one body."],[2220.01,"But you could translate them both in."],[2224.57,"And notice when he's talking about one, he makes clear whether you're Jews or Greeks."],[2231.15,"Jews or Gentiles."],[2232.67,"And also whether slaves or free."],[2252.43,"One of the effects of that that St."],[2255.23,"Paul is mentioning here is that those sort of authority structures that were based in the pagan world."],[2263.28,"Like slavery. And like the separation between Jew and Gentile, those things go away in Christ and in the new community."],[2272.37,"Those things can't be brought in either."],[2277.09,"Okay, that's important."],[2279.01,"And the the best modern day analogy for that would be if people were leaving Hinduism and coming into Christianity, the caste system would have to go too."],[2290.29,"That would be part of that."],[2293.21,"We the the caste system is the probably the closest thing today to understanding the pagan, Roman and Greek view of humanity."],[2301.49,"They thought there were literally better and worse qualities of human."],[2305.25,"Plato lays that out in the Republic"],[2307.93,"But so, and slaves were at the bottom, women were pretty close to the bottom."],[2314.49,"And so sort of free, wealthy men were at the top."],[2320.33,"And so St."],[2321.21,"Paul is saying that goes away."],[2323.69,"Whether it's the pagan version where you've got slaves and free people, these different qualities of humans, or whether it's you're trying to import the Jewish phariseaic version where we're the Jews, you're the no-good, filthy Gentile sinners."],[2340.25,"That can't come in either."],[2342.17,"Neither form of that can come in."],[2344.33,"Because we've all been baptized into Christ and we've all received the same spirit."],[2348.41,"And then in verse 14, for a fact the body is not one member but many."],[2350.17,"the So the same way Christ, our community, which is the body of Christ in world."],[2358.21,"It's not made up of just one big piece where we're all the same."],[2362.59,"But many different pieces."],[2364.19,"Verse 15, if the foot should say, Because I am not a hand, I am not of the body."],[2368.99,"Is it therefore not of the body?"],[2371.64,"So he's saying, so I'm I'm different than the rest of you, so I'm not part of you."],[2376.94,"Now he's not talking about someone who just feels bad and goes and sits in a quarter."],[2382.62,"He's talking about someone who thinks they're better."],[2384.46,"Uh-huh. Or superior to, or doesn't need the body."],[2389.56,"And if the ear should say, because I am not an eye, I am not of the body."],[2392.36,"not of the body Is it therefore?"],[2392.92,"So the same kind of thing"],[2397.12,"Some pieces thinks they're the most important part because they're different."],[2401.52,"And the point St."],[2402.4,"Paul is making here is that no part of the body can separate itself from the body without dying."],[2409.65,"So if the foot is still alive, that means it's still connected to the body."],[2413.57,"No matter how it might want to exalt itself or say it doesn't need it or whatever."],[2418.51,"And the same thing with the ear."],[2420.93,"If they're cut off, they'll die."],[2422.77,"If the whole body were an eye, where would be the hearing?"],[2425.97,"If the whole were hearing, where would be the smelling?"],[2429.59,"So, I mean, this is deliberately comical."],[2433.25,"He's deliberately d being funny here."],[2435.86,"If your whole body was one big eye, how would you hear anything?"],[2440.28,"If your whole body was one big ear, how would you smell anything?"],[2443.96,"I'm thinking of um the cartoon, the nineteenth century cartoon about Emerson."],[2450.2,"Do you know this?"],[2451.08,"The where Emerson in a mystical thing said something like he was a great eyeball."],[2457.16,"And so it shows this this eyeball on legs walking around."],[2463.0,"Uh and uh I mean I think there's some significance to that, and not just because some people uh like Emerson want to reject the physical and just have the spiritual and be you know ex more exalted in that sense."],[2482.08,"And and this cartoon was ridiculing."],[2484.72,"Well, yeah, and I th I think there's a contrast here between the two things he says."],[2489.06,"On the one hand, no part, you know, can is superior to and can survive and thrive by itself."],[2496.1,"On the other hand, all the parts are needed."],[2501.15,"Not just one."],[2502.24,"So neither is one person superior because of what their particular gifts are, where they can go and stand by themselves."],[2510.0,"And on the other hand, everybody shouldn't be trying to all be like the one person who they think has the important gift."],[2520.0,"So, you know, how would this have functioned?"],[2520.32,"you know. Well, not everybody's going to be the priest"],[2527.53,"You can't have, you know, everyone who shows up at church on Sunday be the be priests and no congregation."],[2535.61,"You can't have everybody be chanters."],[2538.89,"You can't have everybody be translators."],[2542.65,"You can't have everybody be people who uh donate for the good of the church, but aren't actually doing the work."],[2552.73,"We couldn't just all show up on Sunday, throw some money in a collection plate and leave."],[2556.83,"That would be a church."],[2560.16,"Because they're very important."],[2562.6,"But somebody has but somebody has to eat."],[2565.28,"What they call."],[2568.66,"And so this is for the whole community to function, you need everybody doing their part with what gifts they have."],[2576.66,"But now God has set the members, each one of them in the body, just as he pleased."],[2582.26,"And if they were all one member, where would the body be?"],[2587.38,"Right if we just had a thousand eyes."],[2592.42,"Verse 20, but now indeed there are many members, yet one body."],[2596.42,"And the eye cannot say to the hand, I have no need of you."],[2599.7,"Nor again the head to the feet, I have no need of you."],[2602.58,"No much rather those members of the body which seem to be weaker are necessary."],[2607.3,"So the thing about the head saying to the feet, I have no need of you."],[2611.42,"It's hard to prove, but that may actually be a reference to Plato."],[2617.5,"Because Plato famously in the in the Timaeus, when he's talking about the creation of humanity, I think it's supposed to be sort of a comical story."],[2626.78,"I don't think he's trying to say it's literally true."],[2629.66,"In that he says that when human beings were originally created, they were just sort of a head."],[2635.07,"To contain their mind."],[2636.6,"He was big into spheres."],[2637.88,"Spheres are sort of a perfect shape."],[2639.82,"So there was just this head sphere."],[2642.8,"And that's what human beings were like."],[2644.48,"But then uh they would roll around and get caught in ditches and in furrows."],[2651.6,"and And so so the demiurge, who was sort of the god who fashioned everything realized this was a problem gave them bodies with legs to carry the head around so that it wouldn't get stuck, wouldn't get stuck anywhere."],[2665.72,"So you you can't prove it, but St."],[2668.6,"Paul may be making a slight allusion to right the head when he says the head can't say to the feet, I don't need you."],[2675.0,"Might be making an allusion to that, that the head would roll around by itself and get stuck somewhere."],[2681.16,"Elizabeth I, once been arguing with her parliament, said it is monstrous for the feet to direct the head."],[2688.44,"Yeah. So then in verse 22, no, much rather those members of the body which seem to be weaker are necessary."],[2696.2,"Yeah, everybody might want to be the head."],[2697.16,"Everybody, everybody wants to be the head, everybody wants to be in charge"],[2703.38,"If you break your foot, you find out pretty fast."],[2705.6,"That you can think of deep thoughts about why you can't go anywhere and you're stuck."],[2710.32,"Yeah. So he's saying, even the the sort of weaker seeming parts."],[2716.85,"If all of a sudden you had no fingers, life would become much more difficult."],[2720.53,"Yeah. Even though you'd say, well, your fingers are or your toes."],[2724.41,"Balancing would suddenly become very difficult."],[2727.51,"So yeah. That's why he's using this analogy."],[2731.53,"So the people you see in the church who you think, you know, oh, whatever they do isn't that important."],[2737.62,"The person who sweeps up."],[2738.74,"The person who cleans the bathroom, you know, does the menial jobs nobody else wants to do"],[2744.28,"In reality, those are just as important, maybe more important than some of the other jobs."],[2750.22,"And so St."],[2750.86,"Paul's saying, whatever it is your gift is, whatever it is that your ministry that you have, whatever activity you're participating in, it's all necessary."],[2759.76,"And it's not that one is one is the one that's really important."],[2763.52,"And oh, sorry for you, that's not the one you got."],[2767.52,"Listen next time as Father Stephen DeYoung continues his study of the scriptures on the Whole Counsel of God."],[2774.8,"Father Stephen's email address is wholecounsel at ancientfaith.com."],[2780.16,"That's whole counsel at ancientface.com.com."]],"o":[{"t":22.24,"lv":2,"h":"Recap: from worship to community","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Picking up at <b>1 Corinthians 12:1</b> - left off early last time: a <b>major break</b>, Paul shifts topics</li><li class=\"d0\">Just finished a large section on <b>worship</b>, mainly the <b>Eucharist</b></li><li class=\"d1\">contrasting (sometimes comparing) church worship with <b>pagan worship</b> in Corinth</li><li class=\"d1\">some Christians still <b>half in pagan, half in Christian</b> world</li><li class=\"d0\">Similarities meant stop: Eucharist and pagan sacrifice both <b>connect a community to a God</b> → can't worship <b>demons and God</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Differences meant stop: <b>sexual immorality</b> common in pagan worship</li><li class=\"d0\">Real <b>separation</b> from city: family gods, city gods, <b>public festivals</b>, even <b>meat</b> from temple sacrifices</li><li class=\"d0\">→ must become <b>their own independent community</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Chapters so far: the <b>negative</b> - things to stop</li><li class=\"d0\">Now the <b>positive</b>: what this new community is like, its <b>identity</b></li><li class=\"d1\">separated from old authority and culture → must form <b>culture and structure</b> to function</li><li class=\"d1\">rest of 1 Corinthians deals with this</li></ul>"},{"t":290.48,"lv":2,"h":"Q&amp;A: Approaching worthily","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Q: what is <b>\"unworthily\"</b>?</li><li class=\"d1\">worthily = approach in <b>repentance</b>, sins confessed, <b>no grievance</b> with another person</li><li class=\"d1\">recognize you're <b>not worthy</b> - adjective sense</li><li class=\"d1\">not \"now I'm holy and pure so I can come\" - <b>think you're holy and pure → not qualified</b></li><li class=\"d1\">come to be <b>purified</b>, to receive Christ</li><li class=\"d0\">Corinth's particular problem: Eucharist in a <b>meal</b>, treated like a <b>pagan feast</b> - drunk, overindulging</li><li class=\"d0\">Joke: increase attendance - <b>potluck</b> = \"the sedate <b>Middle America</b> version of a feast\"</li></ul>"},{"t":387.85,"lv":2,"h":"1 Corinthians 12:1 - Concerning spiritual things","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\"><b>v.1:</b> \"Now concerning spiritual gifts, brethren, I do not want you to be ignorant\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Paul's <b>transition formula</b>: \"now concerning…\"</li><li class=\"d1\">same as start of ch. 8: \"now concerning <b>things offered to idols</b>\"</li></ul>"},{"t":429.15,"lv":3,"h":"\"Gifts\" in italics - the things of the Spirit","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\"><b>\"Gifts\"</b> in italics = <b>not in the Greek</b></li><li class=\"d1\">supplied because that's where Paul goes</li><li class=\"d1\">literally <b>\"spiritual things\"</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Common mistake: almost every time <b>spirit</b> in NT = <b>Holy Spirit</b></li><li class=\"d1\">English <b>\"spiritual\"</b> misleading - religious, pious, no reference to the Spirit</li><li class=\"d1\">\"not religious, <b>spiritual</b>\" - even more confusing</li><li class=\"d1\">OT too: <b>Spirit of God</b></li><li class=\"d1\">exception: another adjective - <b>unclean spirit</b>, <b>lying spirit</b> = demon</li><li class=\"d0\">Better: <b>\"now concerning the things of the Spirit\"</b> - capital S</li><li class=\"d0\">Eucharist: \"what I received from Christ I passed on\" - taught on <b>earlier visits</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Here: something Paul <b>has not taught them</b></li><li class=\"d1\">others (<b>Apollos</b>) have taught there since</li><li class=\"d0\">= \"I need to teach you a little more\" on the Spirit</li></ul>"},{"t":601.78,"lv":2,"h":"12:2 - Carried away to dumb idols","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\"><b>v.2:</b> you were <b>Gentiles</b>, carried away to <b>dumb idols</b></li><li class=\"d1\"><b>dumb</b> = <b>not speaking</b>, not stupid</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>contrast</b>: idols silent vs <b>Holy Spirit who has spoken</b></li><li class=\"d0\"><b>\"However you were led\"</b> = however you ended up there</li><li class=\"d0\">Back to <b>ch. 1-2</b>: Paul lays out themes early</li><li class=\"d1\">they looked to idols, temples, rituals for <b>wisdom</b> - not there</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>all the wisdom of God</b> already given when they received the <b>Holy Spirit</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Idols <b>just sat there</b>, taught nothing</li></ul>"},{"t":689.25,"lv":2,"h":"12:3 - The test: \"Jesus is Lord\"","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\"><b>v.3:</b> no one speaking by the Spirit calls <b>Jesus accursed</b>; no one can say <b>Jesus is Lord</b> except by the <b>Holy Spirit</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Led captive to idols before → <b>a test so you aren't led back</b></li><li class=\"d1\">real wisdom = from the Spirit</li><li class=\"d1\">how to know a teacher speaks by the Spirit? <b>this is how</b></li></ul>"},{"t":747.85,"lv":2,"h":"Q&amp;A: Who says \"Jesus is accursed\"?","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Q: <b>Jesus is God</b>, or Jesus is the <b>authority</b>?</li><li class=\"d0\">Context: why call Jesus accursed? what puts him under a curse? → <b>being crucified</b></li><li class=\"d1\">a <b>particular group of teachers</b> claiming the Spirit</li><li class=\"d1\">not pagans - <b>Jewish teachers</b></li><li class=\"d1\"><b>50s AD</b>: not two religions yet; synagogues mix those who accept Jesus as Messiah and those who don't</li><li class=\"d1\">no \"<b>licensed evangelist</b>\" card from the apostles</li><li class=\"d0\">Paul uses name <b>Jesus</b> = <b>earthly life and ministry</b> (last time: Eucharist, night he was betrayed)</li><li class=\"d1\">accursed: died <b>hanging on a tree</b> (Deuteronomy)</li><li class=\"d1\">that person <b>doesn't have the Spirit</b></li><li class=\"d1\">flip side: says <b>\"Jesus is Lord\"</b> → has the Spirit</li></ul>"},{"t":912.18,"lv":3,"h":"Kyrios = Yahweh","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Not \"Jesus is <b>Christ</b>\" or <b>Messiah</b> - but <b>Lord</b></li><li class=\"d0\"><b><i>Kyrios</i></b> replaces the name <b>Yahweh</b> in Greek OT</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Ch. 8:6</b>: Paul took the <b>Shema</b> - \"Hear O Israel, <b>Yahweh</b> your God, Yahweh is one\"</li><li class=\"d1\">inserted <b>Jesus</b> alongside: \"one God the Father and one <b>Lord Jesus Christ</b>\"</li><li class=\"d0\">→ means: only by the Spirit can anyone acknowledge Jesus is <b>Yahweh, the God of Israel</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Mark of the Spirit: what separates Christians from everyone else is <b>receiving the Holy Spirit</b></li><li class=\"d1\"><b>baptism of the Spirit</b> makes a Christian</li></ul>"},{"t":1011.97,"lv":2,"h":"Peter's confession","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Probably not a reference - <b>Matthew</b> not yet written - but connected idea</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Peter's confession</b>: \"You are the Christ, the Son of the living God\"</li><li class=\"d1\">Jesus: <b>flesh and blood</b> didn't reveal it, but the <b>Holy Spirit</b></li><li class=\"d1\">not whispered in the ear - Spirit <b>allowed you to see</b> who I am</li><li class=\"d0\">\"No one can say it\" ≠ can't get the words out</li><li class=\"d1\">only way to <b>know it true</b> = Spirit dwelling within</li></ul>"},{"t":1096.7,"lv":2,"h":"12:4-6 - Diversities, same Spirit, Lord, God","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\"><b>v.4-6:</b> diversities of <b>gifts</b> - same <b>Spirit</b>; <b>ministries</b> - same <b>Lord</b>; <b>activities</b> - same <b>God</b> who works in all</li><li class=\"d0\">Established: Spirit makes you a member; now <b>not all identical</b></li><li class=\"d1\">different things going on in each</li><li class=\"d0\">Structure = <b>Trinity</b>: Spirit, Lord (Jesus), God the Father</li><li class=\"d1\">Paul <b>clearly Trinitarian</b> over and over - not made up later</li><li class=\"d1\">three in <b>direct parallel</b></li></ul>"},{"t":1181.95,"lv":3,"h":"Activities = energies","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Three things: <b>gifts</b>, <b>ministries</b>, <b>activities</b></li><li class=\"d1\">different <b>abilities</b>, different <b>uses</b> in community</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Activities</b> = same concept as <b>divine energies</b> in Orthodoxy</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>energy</b> literally = <b>in-working</b></li><li class=\"d1\">God at work in <b>all creation</b>, including us; we <b>participate</b></li><li class=\"d1\">in different parts, same God doing all of it</li><li class=\"d0\">Hammered three times: different abilities, <b>one in the same way Father, Son, Spirit are one</b></li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Bird's eye view</b> of the community</li><li class=\"d0\">Student: he says more of this later - \"in a few months\"</li></ul>"},{"t":1299.57,"lv":2,"h":"12:7 - For the profit of all","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\"><b>v.7:</b> manifestation of the Spirit given to each <b>for the profit of all</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Gifts <b>not for my benefit</b></li><li class=\"d1\">not so \"I'm a <b>beautiful and unique snowflake</b>\"</li><li class=\"d1\">given to benefit <b>everyone else</b>; yours likewise</li><li class=\"d1\">by all doing their part the <b>whole community benefits</b></li></ul>"},{"t":1348.98,"lv":3,"h":"12:8 - Wisdom and knowledge","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\"><b>v.8:</b> <b>word of wisdom</b>, <b>word of knowledge</b>, same Spirit</li><li class=\"d0\">Not the same: \"I've got a <b>PhD</b>, I do not have a lot of wisdom\" - wife: \"<b>dumbest smart person</b>\"</li><li class=\"d0\">All real wisdom and knowledge from the <b>Spirit</b></li><li class=\"d1\"><b>knowledge</b>: accumulated to <b>pass on</b>, not to think about how smart you are</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>wisdom</b>: someone with a <b>long, hard life</b>, lessons learned, good <b>advice and discernment</b></li><li class=\"d1\">not to \"sit on a mountain\" with deep thoughts</li></ul>"},{"t":1428.99,"lv":3,"h":"Hermits and the whole church","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Even <b>hermits</b>: <b>St. Anthony</b> took disciples, then passed them on</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>St. Seraphim of Sarov</b> in the woods <b>opened his cell door</b> and received people</li><li class=\"d0\">Even when little contact: <b>correspondence</b>, <b>prayers</b> reach the whole church</li><li class=\"d1\">monasticism <b>not an exception</b> - gifts given for the <b>whole church</b></li><li class=\"d1\">God gives <b>saints</b> as gifts to the church</li></ul>"},{"t":1499.77,"lv":2,"h":"12:9-11 - Other gifts","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\"><b>v.9-10:</b> <b>faith</b>, <b>healings</b>, <b>miracles</b>, <b>prophecy</b>, <b>discerning of spirits</b>, <b>kinds of tongues</b>, <b>interpretation of tongues</b></li><li class=\"d0\"><b>v.11:</b> one and the same Spirit, <b>distributing as he wills</b></li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Faith</b> = more <b>faithfulness</b></li><li class=\"d1\">not \"that guy believes really hard\"</li><li class=\"d1\">faithfulness of life - <b>martyrs</b> faithful unto death</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>wonder-working</b> saints</li></ul>"},{"t":1554.32,"lv":2,"h":"Tongues = languages","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Q: tongues - translators (<b>KJV</b>, even Orthodox Study Bible) say <b>\"tongues\"</b></li><li class=\"d1\">literal Greek, but means <b>languages</b></li><li class=\"d1\">gift for <b>speaking many languages</b>; <b>interpretation</b> = translating - separate skills</li><li class=\"d0\">Others read it as <b>incomprehensible speech</b> + interpreter - \"like the <b>oracle at Delphi</b>\"</li><li class=\"d1\">Paul's thrust: gifts for <b>everyone else's benefit</b></li><li class=\"d1\">babbling then translated - \"<b>parlor trick</b>\"? how does that help the community?</li><li class=\"d1\">other gifts clearly help; languages and translating clearly help</li></ul>"},{"t":1663.83,"lv":3,"h":"Linguistic diversity of the world","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\"><b>Corinth</b>: port, land route between Peloponnese and mainland</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>Latin</b>-speaking Romans, <b>Greek</b> main, <b>Aramaic</b> speakers of Jewish background, <b>local languages</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Aramaic known from Gospels (Galilee, Judea) - but <b>native languages everywhere</b></li><li class=\"d1\">Egypt: common people spoke <b>Egyptian</b> (→ <b>Coptic</b>); Aramaic → <b>Syriac</b></li><li class=\"d1\">wealthy and Romans spoke <b>Greek</b></li><li class=\"d1\"><b>Tatian</b> calls the Roman ruling class \"the Greeks\"</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>Galatia</b>: Roman settlers spoke Greek, natives <b>own language</b> - \"reading Galatians soon\"</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Cosmopolitan cities</b> (Corinth, Alexandria, Jerusalem, Rome, Antioch)</li><li class=\"d1\">speaking to visitors, <b>translating</b>, <b>preaching in local language</b> = very useful</li><li class=\"d0\">Educated knew Greek; early converts <b>mostly women and slaves</b> - no formal education → important gift</li></ul>"},{"t":1824.44,"lv":3,"h":"Translation, Pentecost, and miracle","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\"><b>Eucharist service</b> in Greek (majority language) → others needing <b>translation and explanation</b></li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Not always non-miraculous</b>: example of miraculous language = <b>Pentecost</b></li><li class=\"d1\">apostles preached, <b>everyone understood</b></li><li class=\"d1\">\"the <b>exact opposite</b> of what people call speaking in tongues today\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Could include miracle: speaking a language <b>you didn't know</b> to communicate the gospel</li><li class=\"d1\">but not a \"made up fictional language\"</li><li class=\"d1\">\"alienated my probably <b>non-existent Pentecostal audience</b>\" </li><li class=\"d0\"><b>v.11:</b> same Spirit gives to each <b>individually as he wills</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Why: Corinth's background of <b>initiation experiences</b>, <b>mysteries of Demeter</b>, to acquire spiritual knowledge</li><li class=\"d1\">not ascending to the next level of <b>Thetans</b> in <b>Scientology</b> - \"like Tom Cruise\"</li><li class=\"d1\">Spirit <b>gives</b> as he wills</li></ul>"},{"t":1977.27,"lv":2,"h":"12:12 - One body, many members","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\"><b>v.12:</b> body one, many members, all one body - <b>\"so also is Christ\"</b></li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Protracted analogy</b>: different parts, all one body</li><li class=\"d1\">\"my big toe doesn't look like my nose, thankfully\"</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Shift</b>: not \"so is the church\" but <b>\"so also is Christ\"</b></li><li class=\"d1\">introduces <b>church as the body of Christ</b></li></ul>"},{"t":2055.58,"lv":2,"h":"12:13 - Baptized into one body","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\"><b>v.13:</b> by one Spirit <b>baptized into one body</b>, Jews or Greeks, slaves or free; <b>made to drink into one Spirit</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Water baptism → <b>Holy Spirit</b> immediately after (as with Moses, ch. 10)</li><li class=\"d1\">receiving the Spirit makes you <b>Christian</b></li><li class=\"d1\">why <b>baptism and chrismation</b> stay united in Orthodoxy; West separated → <b>confirmation</b> later</li></ul>"},{"t":2101.71,"lv":3,"h":"Aside: \"into\" and Istanbul","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Q: \"drink <b>into</b> one Spirit\" - strange</li><li class=\"d1\">Greek <i>eis</i> can mean <b>in</b> or <b>to</b>; shifting in 1st c. <b>in → into → to</b></li><li class=\"d1\">translators being <b>literal</b>; could translate both \"in\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Trivia: <i>eis tēn polin</i> = <b>\"to the city\"</b> → <b>Istanbul</b></li><li class=\"d1\">Turk asked where Constantinople was; P to B shift</li></ul>"},{"t":2224.57,"lv":2,"h":"Jew/Greek, slave/free - authority structures gone","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\"><b>Jews or Gentiles</b>, <b>slaves or free</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Pagan authority structures <b>can't be brought in</b></li><li class=\"d1\"><b>slavery</b>; Jew-Gentile separation</li><li class=\"d0\">Modern analogy: leaving <b>Hinduism</b> → <b>caste system</b> has to go too</li><li class=\"d1\">closest to Greek and Roman view: <b>better and worse qualities of human</b> - <b>Plato</b>, *Republic*</li><li class=\"d1\">slaves at the bottom, women near, <b>free wealthy men</b> at top</li><li class=\"d0\">Neither version: <b>pagan</b> slave/free, or <b>Pharisaic</b> \"we're the Jews, you're <b>filthy Gentile sinners</b>\"</li><li class=\"d1\">all baptized into Christ, <b>same Spirit</b></li></ul>"},{"t":2348.41,"lv":2,"h":"12:14-17 - Foot, ear, eye","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\"><b>v.14:</b> body not one member but <b>many</b></li><li class=\"d0\"><b>v.15:</b> foot: \"because I am not a hand, I am not of the body\"</li><li class=\"d1\">not someone who feels bad - someone who thinks they're <b>better</b>, doesn't need the body</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>no part can separate</b> without <b>dying</b> - if the foot is alive, it's <b>still connected</b></li><li class=\"d0\"><b>v.16-17:</b> ear, eye</li><li class=\"d1\">whole body an eye → where's the <b>hearing</b>? deliberately <b>comical</b></li></ul>"},{"t":2422.77,"lv":3,"h":"Eyeball cartoon and the two sides","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Student: <b>Emerson</b> cartoon - \"great <b>eyeball</b>\" on legs - mocking rejection of the physical</li><li class=\"d0\">Two sides of Paul's point</li><li class=\"d1\">no part <b>superior</b>, can thrive alone</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>all parts needed</b>; not everyone trying to be the one with the \"important\" gift</li><li class=\"d0\">How it functions: not everybody the <b>priest</b>, <b>chanters</b>, <b>translators</b></li><li class=\"d1\">not everyone \"<b>donate</b> but not do the work\"</li><li class=\"d1\">can't all <b>throw money in the plate</b> and leave</li><li class=\"d1\">everyone doing <b>their part</b> with their gifts</li></ul>"},{"t":2576.66,"lv":2,"h":"12:18-22 - Head and feet","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\"><b>v.18-19:</b> God set the members <b>as he pleased</b>; if all one member, where's the body? - \"a thousand eyes\"</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>v.20-22:</b> many members, one body</li><li class=\"d1\">eye can't say to hand \"<b>I have no need of you</b>\"; head to feet</li><li class=\"d1\">members that seem <b>weaker</b> are <b>necessary</b></li></ul>"},{"t":2611.42,"lv":2,"h":"Plato's Timaeus and the weaker members","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Possible <b>allusion to Plato</b>, *Timaeus* (hard to prove)</li><li class=\"d1\">humans first created as a <b>head sphere</b>, rolled into ditches</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>demiurge</b> gave bodies with <b>legs</b> to carry it</li><li class=\"d1\">head can't say to feet <b>\"I don't need you\"</b></li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Elizabeth I</b> to Parliament: \"<b>monstrous</b> for the feet to direct the head\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Everyone wants to be the <b>head</b>, in charge</li><li class=\"d1\">break a foot → stuck with deep thoughts</li><li class=\"d1\">no fingers, or toes (<b>balance</b>) → life harder</li><li class=\"d0\">Seemingly unimportant: person who <b>sweeps</b>, <b>cleans the bathroom</b>, menial jobs</li><li class=\"d1\">as important, <b>maybe more</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Whatever <b>gift, ministry, activity</b> → <b>all necessary</b>; not \"the one that's really important\"</li></ul>"}]});
